INT. MATTRESS SHOWROOM - 3 A.M.

Fluorescent lights. Forty beds, no customers. Soft jazz.

A WOMAN in a nightgown and parka lies on a king-size display model, hands folded on her chest. She stares at the ceiling.

DWAYNE, 30s, the night salesman, stands at the foot of the bed with a clipboard.

DWAYNE
Ma'am, we're a mattress store.

WOMAN
I know what you are.

DWAYNE
You can't sleep here.

WOMAN
I'm testing.

DWAYNE
For six hours?

WOMAN
It's a firm medium. It takes time.

He writes something. Crosses it out.

DWAYNE
What's your name?

WOMAN
Priya.

DWAYNE
Priya, my manager is going to be here at four.

PRIYA
Then I have an hour.

She rolls over. Pulls a pillow to her face.

The front door chimes. MS. LOCKHART, 50s, regional manager in a camel coat, enters with a travel mug. She sees the bed. Sees Priya. Sees Dwayne.

MS. LOCKHART
Dwayne.

DWAYNE
It's not what it looks like.

MS. LOCKHART
It looks like a customer is asleep.

DWAYNE
Then it's exactly what it looks like.

Lockhart sips her coffee. Sits on the next bed over. Kicks off her heels.

MS. LOCKHART
Scoot.
